Your terminal is running adb wait-for-device && adb root && adb remount, which means it is waiting for the emulator to finish booting before executing adb root and adb remount.
However, notice the emulator window on the left is still white/blank—when -writable-system is combined with -gpu angle_indirect or swiftshader_indirect on API 30 after disable-verity, Android's overlayfs setup during boot sometimes freezes the GUI if hardware virtualization (/dev/kvm) or RAM is tight, or it is still finishing its boot sequence.

How to Check What Is Happening Right Now

1. Give it 1 more minute: If the terminal suddenly prints restarting adbd as root and remount succeeded (or Now reboot your device...), it finished booting!
2. If it stays stuck on wait-for-device after 2 minutes: Open a third terminal tab (Ctrl + Shift + T) and run:bash
~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb devices
Use code with caution.

	• If adb devices lists nothing at all (or offline indefinitely), angle_indirect + -writable-system stalled during early kernel boot.

Guaranteed Workaround If -writable-system Keeps Stalling on Boot

On Android 11 (API 30) with google_apis, you actually do not need -writable-system or adb remount at all to install a system CA certificate! Because adb root works out-of-the-box on a google_apis image even in normal mode, you can mount a temporary in-memory tmpfs over /system/etc/security/cacerts without modifying the system image or disabling verity:
1. Boot the emulator normally (fast, no -writable-system):bash
pkill -9 -f qemu-system; rm -rf ~/.android/avd/Pixel_5.avd/*.lock
~/Android/Sdk/emulator/emulator -avd Pixel_5 -gpu swiftshader_indirect
Use code with caution.
2. Once it boots to the home screen, inject the PortSwigger certificate into /system/etc/security/cacerts via tmpfs:bash
~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb root
~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb shell "mkdir -p /data/local/tmp/cacerts && cp /system/etc/security/cacerts/* /data/local/tmp/cacerts/ && cp /data/misc/user/0/cacerts-added/* /data/local/tmp/cacerts/ && mount -t tmpfs tmpfs /system/etc/security/cacerts && cp /data/local/tmp/cacerts/* /system/etc/security/cacerts/ && chmod 644 /system/etc/security/cacerts/* && chcon u:object_r:system_file:s0 /system/etc/security/cacerts/*"
Use code with caution.
(This takes 2 seconds, works immediately without rebooting, and makes Android trust your PortSwigger certificate as a system CA).
